<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fire temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fire temples</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A fire temple is a place of worship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, where a sacred fire is kept burning. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, Zoroastrianism was closely linked to the state, and great fire temples, some associated with the monarchy, were important religious and political centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fire-temple.jpg" alt="Fire temple" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploaded to Flickr by Adam Jones(Copied to Wikimedia by nagualdesign) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zoroastrian_Fire_Temple,_Yazd_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fire rituals are first evident in the 9th century BCE, about the same time as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> itself and the introduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a> as a divinity. There is no allusion to a temple of fire in the Avesta proper, nor an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> word for one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> shows that in the mid-5th century BCE Zoroastrians worshipped to the open sky, ascending mounds to light their fires, so the temple is a later development. The oldest remains identified as a fire temple are on Mount Khajeh, near Lake Hamun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, tentatively dated to the 3rd or 4th century BCE, rebuilt in the Parthian era and enlarged in Sassanid times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the Parthian period there were two kinds of place of worship, the bagin, a sanctuary of a particular divinity with an icon, and the atroshan, a place of burning fire, which became more common as the movement against images gained support. Under the Sassanid dynasty the statues were by law abandoned or replaced by fire altars. There seem to have been virtually only two grades of fire, the Atash-i Vahram, kept continuously burning, and the Atash-i Adaran, a parish fire relit each year. The characteristic Sassanid sanctuary was a domed chamber on a square plan with a pillar at each corner, and Sassanid coins always show a fire altar with flames on the reverse.</p><p>Three Great Fires, Adur Burzen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mihr</a>, Adur Farnbag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp">Adur Gushnasp</a>, were said to derive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> and were associated with priests, warriors and farmers; other texts call them symbols of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The battles of al-Qādisiyyah in 636 and Nihavānd in 642 were instrumental in the collapse of the Sassanid Empire and of state-sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, and some fire temples were destroyed or converted into mosques, though others continued. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> legend, refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> brought the ash of a fire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Its fire was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a> to the Bahrot Caves, then to Bansdah and Navsari, and in the 18th century to Udvada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Fire temples are built to serve the fire within them and are classified by its grade: Atash Dadgah, Atash Adaran and Atash Behram. A Dadgah fire can be consecrated in a few hours by two priests, an Adaran fire requires eight priests and two to three weeks, and a Behram fire gathers sixteen kinds of fire and requires thirty-two priests for a ceremony that can take up to a year. The outer façade of a temple is almost always plain. Only priests may enter the innermost sanctum, which has a double domed roof and no light but the fire's. Worshippers offer dry sandalwood through a priest, who does not preach but tends the fire. Priests hold the ranks of dastur, mobad and herbad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fire temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Where the Arsacids had ruled through a loose confederacy of semi-autonomous noble houses, Ardashir pursued centralisation: he curbed the great clans, reorganised the administration of the Iranian plateau and made Zoroastrianism the state-sponsored religion, supported by a network of royal <b>fire temples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fire temples</b> keep sacred fires burning continuously, the most important of which have burned for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings ruled from Ganzak, near the later <b>fire temple</b> of Takht-e Soleyman, over a land of horse pastures and mountains.</blockquote><a href="/state/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion was a local Zoroastrianism with <b>fire temples</b> and ossuary burial, alongside Buddhism, Manichaeism and Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-samarkand">Principality of Samarkand</a></li></ul></section>
